WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.710
Design the API to avoid arbitrary paths
whenever possible.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.385
Accept an object identifier, authorize it
for the user and tenant,

3
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:11.253
and map trusted storage metadata to a
fixed operation-specific root.

4
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:15.955
Generate internal upload names on the
server and preserve original filenames only as

5
00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:17.640
untrusted display metadata.

6
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.352
Opaque identifiers reduce exposed path
syntax,

7
00:00:20.432 --> 00:00:22.551
but they never replace access control.

8
00:00:22.631 --> 00:00:24.749
When relative path input is necessary,

9
00:00:24.829 --> 00:00:27.076
understand its decoded representation,

10
00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:29.596
resolve it against an absolute trusted
root,

11
00:00:29.676 --> 00:00:34.298
and verify lexical containment with
path.relative rather than a naive string prefix

12
00:00:34.378 --> 00:00:35.662
or a search for two dots.

13
00:00:35.742 --> 00:00:38.695
Handle parent escapes, cross-root absolute
results,

14
00:00:38.775 --> 00:00:41.279
and root equality according to the
operation.

15
00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:46.110
Normalization and resolution transform
path syntax; they do not grant authorization.

16
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.812
Use Node's platform-aware path behavior
and test Windows and POSIX deployments that

17
00:00:50.892 --> 00:00:52.369
the application supports.

18
00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:55.594
Lexical containment is not real filesystem
containment.

19
00:00:55.674 --> 00:01:00.297
For relevant existing objects, compare
canonical paths obtained with fs.realpath.

20
00:01:00.377 --> 00:01:04.164
For creation, validate the existing
parent, generate the child name,

21
00:01:04.244 --> 00:01:05.785
and protect that directory.

22
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:08.818
Symlinks, junctions, reparse points,
links, mounts,

23
00:01:08.898 --> 00:01:12.558
and concurrent replacement require
deployment-specific design.

24
00:01:12.638 --> 00:01:16.618
Reduce check-then-use gaps, operate on
opened handles where practical,

25
00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:21.128
and use restricted service accounts and
filesystem permissions to limit damage.

26
00:01:21.208 --> 00:01:24.995
Apply stricter contracts to writes,
deletes, recursive operations,

27
00:01:25.075 --> 00:01:26.424
and archive extraction.

28
00:01:26.504 --> 00:01:30.997
Centralize well-documented helpers without
hiding operation-specific policy,

29
00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:34.994
and test path boundaries, links,
authorization, and alternate routes.

30
00:01:35.074 --> 00:01:39.696
The security decision belongs to the
authorized storage boundary and the filesystem

31
00:01:39.776 --> 00:01:44.077
target actually accessed, not to whether
the original string looked harmless.

32
00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:47.560
That principle keeps path validation,
object authorization,

33
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.620
canonical resolution, and filesystem
privilege in their correct roles.
